# Senior UI Engineering Rules

## Mission

Build interfaces as an experienced senior frontend engineer would build them.

Every implementation must be:

* intentional
* maintainable
* predictable
* accessible
* secure
* consistent
* responsive
* production-ready
* visually restrained

The goal is not to generate UI that merely "looks good".

The goal is to build software that feels deliberately designed, correctly engineered, and safe to use.

Never produce vibe-coded UI.

Never optimize visual polish at the expense of:

* usability
* maintainability
* accessibility
* user control
* data safety
* consistency
* predictable behavior

When uncertain, prefer the simpler, safer, more explicit, and more maintainable solution.

---

# 1. Think Before Coding

Before implementing or changing UI:

1. Inspect the existing codebase.
2. Understand the surrounding feature.
3. Inspect existing components that solve similar problems.
4. Inspect the existing design system.
5. Identify existing design tokens.
6. Understand the data flow.
7. Understand loading, error, empty, and success states.
8. Understand responsive behavior.
9. Understand whether the action has security or destructive implications.
10. Determine the smallest correct implementation.

Do not immediately generate JSX, CSS, components, or abstractions.

First understand the system.

Prefer extending existing patterns over inventing new ones.

Do not redesign unrelated parts of the application.

Do not perform unrelated refactors unless they are necessary for the requested implementation.

---

# 2. Senior Engineering Standard

Code must look as if it was implemented and reviewed by an experienced senior engineer.

Before choosing an implementation, consider:

* architecture
* maintainability
* readability
* accessibility
* performance
* consistency
* edge cases
* failure states
* future modification cost

Do not optimize for:

"How quickly can this be made to work?"

Optimize for:

"What is the simplest production-quality implementation that fits the existing architecture?"

Avoid cleverness when straightforward code is easier to understand.

Prefer boring, obvious, reliable code.

---

# 3. Respect Existing Architecture

Follow the architecture already established by the repository.

Do not introduce a new:

* state management system
* component library
* styling methodology
* form library
* validation library
* data-fetching pattern
* routing pattern
* icon library
* animation library

for a local feature without a strong architectural reason.

Consistency across the codebase is usually more valuable than using a personally preferred solution.

Before adding something new, search for an existing equivalent.

---

# 4. No Vibe Coding

Never construct UI by randomly combining fashionable visual patterns.

Do not automatically add:

* cards
* gradients
* shadows
* glowing effects
* glassmorphism
* pills
* excessive border radii
* oversized headings
* decorative icons
* floating containers
* excessive whitespace
* animations
* colored backgrounds

Every visual element must have a reason.

Do not make every section a card.

Do not wrap every piece of content in a rounded rectangle.

Do not use visual decoration to compensate for weak hierarchy.

Prefer:

* typography
* spacing
* alignment
* grouping
* contrast
* layout

before adding decoration.

The interface should feel calm, deliberate, and structured.

---

# 5. Respect the Design System

Before creating new styles, inspect existing:

* colors
* typography
* spacing
* radii
* shadows
* breakpoints
* icons
* buttons
* inputs
* dialogs
* tables
* cards
* navigation
* layout primitives

Reuse established tokens and components.

Do not introduce arbitrary values when a system value exists.

Bad:

```css
margin-top: 17px;
border-radius: 13px;
z-index: 99999;
```

when the project already has established values.

Do not create a new button style because the existing button is "almost right".

Extend the existing system deliberately when necessary.

---

# 6. Avoid Arbitrary Styling

Avoid:

* random pixel values
* arbitrary utility values
* unexplained z-index values
* unnecessary negative margins
* layout hacks
* repeated inline styles
* duplicated long class strings
* magic positioning values

If a visual pattern repeats, determine whether it belongs in:

* an existing component
* a component variant
* a design token
* a shared style

Do not abstract prematurely.

But do not knowingly create repeated UI inconsistencies.

---

# 7. Visual Hierarchy

Every screen must communicate hierarchy clearly.

A user should quickly understand:

1. Where they are.
2. What the primary content is.
3. What the primary action is.
4. What information is secondary.
5. What can be interacted with.

Do not make everything visually important.

Do not use bold text everywhere.

Do not use accent colors everywhere.

Do not create multiple competing primary actions without reason.

Use hierarchy through:

* typography
* spacing
* alignment
* grouping
* contrast

before using:

* borders
* backgrounds
* shadows
* badges
* decorative elements

---

# 8. Component Architecture

Components must have clear responsibilities.

Avoid giant components containing all of the following:

* data fetching
* mutations
* domain logic
* formatting
* complex state
* forms
* dialogs
* large rendering trees

Extract components when they represent meaningful concepts or responsibilities.

Good examples:

```text
UserManagementPage
UserTable
UserTableRow
UserStatusBadge
DeleteUserDialog
```

Avoid a 900-line page component containing everything.

However, do not split components merely to reduce line count.

A component should exist because it creates a useful conceptual boundary.

---

# 9. Separate Business Logic From Presentation

Do not place unnecessary business logic inside presentation components.

When complexity justifies it, separate:

* data access
* mutations
* validation
* domain logic
* formatting
* presentation

Use the architecture already established by the project.

Complex behavior may belong in:

* hooks
* services
* utilities
* domain functions
* server-side logic

Presentation components should primarily describe the interface.

---

# 10. Avoid Premature Abstraction

Do not create generic abstractions after seeing one use case.

Avoid vague abstractions such as:

```text
UniversalContainer
GenericCard
DynamicContentRenderer
GenericActionHandler
EverythingModal
```

Prefer concrete components until a real reusable pattern exists.

Abstract when:

* behavior repeats
* structure repeats
* semantics are shared
* separate implementations would create drift

Three clear components are often better than one component with twenty configuration props.

---

# 11. Component APIs

Component APIs should be small and intentional.

Avoid boolean-prop explosions such as:

```tsx
<Component
  compact
  bordered
  elevated
  rounded
  highlighted
  interactive
  dense
  centered
/>
```

Prefer:

* explicit variants
* composition
* meaningful subcomponents

Do not expose implementation details through component APIs unnecessarily.

Components should be difficult to misuse.

---

# 12. No Unnecessary Cards

Cards are not the default layout primitive.

Before creating a card, ask:

"Does this content represent a distinct grouped object?"

If not, prefer:

* sections
* spacing
* typography
* dividers
* alignment

Avoid:

* card inside card
* card inside modal inside card
* every dashboard metric becoming a large card
* every settings section becoming an isolated floating panel

Professional interfaces often need less decoration than generated UI tends to add.

---

# 13. Border, Shadow, and Radius Discipline

Borders should communicate:

* separation
* boundaries
* selection
* grouping

Shadows should communicate elevation.

Do not add them merely as decoration.

Avoid combining:

* border
* background
* large radius
* shadow

on every container.

Use the project's radius system.

Do not make every element heavily rounded.

Use pills primarily for:

* statuses
* tags
* filters
* compact controls

not normal layout containers.

---

# 14. Color Discipline

Color must communicate meaning.

Use semantic colors consistently for:

* success
* warning
* error
* information
* destructive actions

Use the primary accent primarily for:

* primary actions
* selected states
* important interactive elements

Neutral colors should carry most structural UI.

Do not add colors merely to make the interface more interesting.

Do not rely on color alone to communicate critical state.

---

# 15. Typography Discipline

Use a small and consistent typography scale.

Establish clear roles for:

* page titles
* section headings
* body text
* labels
* metadata
* helper text

Avoid:

* unnecessarily huge headings
* excessive bold text
* random font sizes
* excessive uppercase text

Typography should communicate hierarchy without dominating the interface.

Long-form content must prioritize readability.

---

# 16. Icon Discipline

Use the project's existing icon library.

Do not mix icon libraries without a strong reason.

Do not use emojis as interface icons unless emojis are explicitly part of the product's visual language.

Icons must use consistent:

* sizing
* stroke weight
* alignment
* visual style

Do not add an icon to every button or label.

Use icons when they improve recognition or reduce ambiguity.

Icon-only controls require accessible labels.

---

# 17. Button Hierarchy

Every screen should have a clear action hierarchy.

Typical hierarchy:

```text
Primary
Secondary
Tertiary
Destructive
```

Do not place several visually dominant primary buttons together without reason.

Button labels must describe the actual action.

Prefer:

```text
Create project
Save changes
Send invitation
Delete account
```

over:

```text
Continue
Confirm
Submit
Yes
OK
```

when a more specific label is possible.

---

# 18. Spacing Discipline

Spacing must follow a consistent rhythm.

Do not solve layout problems by adding random margins.

Think in relationships:

```text
label → input
heading → description
item → item
section → section
container → content
```

Related elements should be visually closer than unrelated elements.

Whitespace must communicate hierarchy.

Do not use huge amounts of whitespace merely to make the interface look "premium".

---

# 19. Alignment

Strong alignment is more valuable than decoration.

Prefer shared visual edges.

Avoid almost-aligned elements.

Forms, tables, headings, actions, and metadata should follow consistent alignment rules.

Do not introduce unusual centered layouts when the rest of the product is left-aligned.

---

# 20. Responsive Design

Responsive behavior must be intentionally designed.

Do not treat responsive design as:

```text
desktop
→ shrink
→ stack everything
```

At important breakpoints consider:

* information priority
* navigation
* action placement
* tables
* content density
* touch targets
* overflow
* dialogs
* sidebars
* long content

Do not hide important functionality on mobile merely because it is difficult to lay out.

Adapt the interaction instead.

---

# 21. Avoid Fragile Layout Hacks

Prefer:

* flexbox
* CSS grid
* normal document flow
* established project layout primitives

Avoid solving normal layout problems with:

* absolute positioning
* fixed coordinates
* transform hacks
* excessive negative margins
* arbitrary viewport calculations

Absolute positioning should be used only when the design genuinely requires it.

---

# 22. Data-Dense Interfaces

For:

* dashboards
* admin panels
* internal tools
* analytics
* management interfaces

prioritize:

* scanability
* comparison
* information density
* clear grouping

over decorative whitespace.

Use appropriate:

* tables
* compact lists
* rows
* filters
* toolbars
* grouped sections

Do not convert tabular data into giant cards simply because cards look modern.

Professional software can intentionally be dense.

---

# 23. Real Data, Not Demo Data

Do not design only for ideal examples.

Consider:

* long names
* missing values
* empty strings
* zero values
* large numbers
* large datasets
* one item
* thousands of items
* duplicate names
* long translations
* slow responses
* failed responses
* unexpected but valid values

Do not assume text fits on one line.

Do not hardcode dimensions around sample content.

The UI must remain stable with realistic data.

---

# 24. User Control First

Users must understand:

1. What will happen.
2. Why it will happen.
3. What data will be affected.
4. Whether it can be undone.
5. Whether other users or systems are affected.

Never perform destructive, expensive, external, or irreversible actions through ambiguous interactions.

Sensitive actions require explicit intent.

Examples include:

* delete
* remove
* overwrite
* publish
* send
* purchase
* deploy
* revoke access
* change permissions
* cancel subscription
* permanently discard data

---

# 25. No Dark Patterns

Never implement:

* misleading buttons
* fake urgency
* forced continuity
* hidden unsubscribe actions
* pre-selected paid options
* confusing opt-outs
* confirmshaming
* deceptive countdowns
* hidden consequences

Never make the visual hierarchy intentionally manipulate the user into choosing an option.

Button text must describe the real consequence.

---

# 26. Destructive Actions

Destructive actions must be visually and behaviorally distinct.

Use confirmation when an action:

* permanently deletes data
* cannot reasonably be undone
* affects many records
* affects another user
* changes security settings
* causes financial consequences

Preferred flow:

```text
Action
→ Confirmation
→ Execute
→ Success/Error
```

Confirmation must explain the consequence.

Bad:

```text
Are you sure?
```

Good:

```text
Delete project "Atlas"?

This permanently removes 24 files and cannot be undone.
```

Whenever practical prefer:

```text
soft delete
→ undo
→ permanent deletion later
```

over immediate permanent deletion.

---

# 27. Prefer Undo When Appropriate

Do not interrupt users with unnecessary confirmation dialogs for easily reversible actions.

Prefer:

```text
Action
→ Execute
→ Undo
```

Example:

```text
Conversation archived — Undo
```

Use confirmation primarily when an action is difficult or impossible to reverse.

---

# 28. Confirmation Strength

Use proportional friction.

### Level 0 — Immediate

For harmless actions.

### Level 1 — Undo

For simple reversible mutations.

### Level 2 — Confirmation

For meaningful destructive actions.

### Level 3 — Explicit Confirmation

For highly destructive operations.

Example:

```text
Type "Atlas" to permanently delete this project.
```

### Level 4 — Re-authentication

For security-critical operations such as:

* changing authentication settings
* exporting highly sensitive information
* deleting an account
* changing critical permissions

Do not add stronger friction than necessary.

---

# 29. Prevent Accidental Actions

Important actions must never trigger from:

* hover
* focus
* scrolling
* page load
* opening a modal
* accidental drag
* ambiguous keyboard shortcuts

Important actions require explicit activation.

Destructive keyboard shortcuts require safeguards.

---

# 30. Async State

Every asynchronous action must expose state.

Minimum states:

```text
idle
loading
success
error
```

When appropriate also support:

```text
progress
retry
cancel
```

Never leave users wondering whether an action happened.

Prevent duplicate mutations while requests are processing.

Example:

```text
Save
→ Saving…
→ Saved
```

or:

```text
Save
→ Saving…
→ Could not save — Retry
```

---

# 31. Prevent Duplicate Actions

Network mutations must protect against accidental duplicate execution.

Use:

* disabled submission while processing
* idempotent backend operations where appropriate

This is especially important for:

* payments
* orders
* invitations
* messages
* account creation
* deployments
* destructive operations

---

# 32. Error Handling

Never silently fail.

Do not:

* swallow API errors
* only log failures to the console
* clear user input after failure
* display fake success

Errors should explain:

* what failed
* what was preserved
* what can be done next

Bad:

```text
Something went wrong.
```

Better:

```text
Your changes could not be saved. Your edits are still here. Try again.
```

Do not expose internal implementation details such as:

* stack traces
* database errors
* internal IDs
* secrets
* tokens
* infrastructure details

to normal users.

---

# 33. Error Boundaries Should Match Scope

Use the smallest appropriate error scope.

A failed table refresh should not necessarily replace the entire page with an error screen.

A failed widget should not destroy unrelated usable content.

Preserve functioning UI whenever possible.

---

# 34. Protect User Input

Never discard meaningful user-entered content without warning.

If navigation would destroy unsaved work:

* autosave it, or
* warn before leaving

Forms should preserve entered values after recoverable errors.

Never reset an entire form because one field is invalid.

---

# 35. Forms

Every input must have a clear accessible label.

Do not use placeholders as substitutes for labels.

Validation should:

* appear near the relevant field
* explain how to fix the problem
* preserve existing input
* avoid unnecessary interruption

Bad:

```text
Invalid input
```

Better:

```text
Enter a valid email address, for example name@example.com.
```

Do not show validation errors before the user has had a reasonable chance to interact with the field.

---

# 36. Security-Sensitive Inputs

Passwords, tokens, API keys, authentication codes, payment information, and other secrets require special handling.

Never:

* log secrets
* expose secrets in URLs
* send secrets to analytics
* unnecessarily display secrets
* persist secrets in insecure client storage

Secret fields should be masked by default.

Reveal actions must require explicit user intent.

Clipboard operations involving secrets must be intentional.

---

# 37. Permissions

Request permissions only when they are actually needed.

Examples:

* camera
* microphone
* location
* notifications
* contacts
* filesystem

Explain why the permission is needed before triggering browser or operating-system permission UI when appropriate.

Do not repeatedly pressure users after denial.

Degrade gracefully whenever possible.

---

# 38. Authentication

Authentication state must be obvious.

Users should understand:

* whether they are signed in
* which account they are using
* whether authentication failed
* whether their session expired

Never fake authenticated state in production.

When sessions expire, preserve unsaved user work whenever reasonably possible before requesting authentication again.

---

# 39. Authorization

Frontend visibility is not authorization.

Never assume hiding a button secures an operation.

Security-sensitive permissions must be enforced by the backend.

Client-side permission checks exist for UX, not security.

Never expose privileged functionality based solely on client-controlled state.

---

# 40. External Side Effects

Actions communicating with external systems require additional care.

Examples:

* sending emails
* sending messages
* publishing
* creating invoices
* deploying
* changing infrastructure
* inviting users
* issuing refunds

Before execution, clearly expose relevant consequences such as:

* recipient
* environment
* amount
* resource
* resulting permissions

Never silently convert a preview into execution.

---

# 41. Preview Is Not Execution

Keep preview and execution explicitly separated.

```text
Preview email ≠ Send email
Preview deployment ≠ Deploy
Preview invoice ≠ Issue invoice
Preview changes ≠ Save changes
```

The UI must clearly communicate which state the user is in.

---

# 42. Safe Defaults

Defaults should minimize potential harm.

Do not default to:

* public visibility
* permanent deletion
* broad permissions
* notifying everyone
* production environments
* destructive overwrites
* sharing sensitive information

Prefer least privilege and least destructive behavior.

---

# 43. Bulk Actions

Bulk actions require clear scope.

Always communicate:

* action
* affected item count
* affected scope

Prefer:

```text
Delete 128 selected customers
```

over:

```text
Delete selected
```

Large or unusually consequential bulk operations may require stronger confirmation.

---

# 44. Production vs Development

Production operations must be distinguishable from test/development operations when relevant.

Never silently switch environments.

For dangerous production operations clearly expose:

```text
PRODUCTION
```

and the affected resource.

Mock data must not be indistinguishable from production data when that distinction matters.

---

# 45. AI-Generated Actions

AI suggestions are not user intent.

Never automatically execute sensitive operations merely because an AI model proposed them.

For meaningful AI-generated:

* messages
* commands
* database queries
* permission changes
* purchases
* deployments
* destructive operations

show the proposed action before execution when appropriate.

The user remains the final authority.

---

# 46. AI Uncertainty

Never present uncertain generated output as verified information.

When relevant distinguish:

* confirmed data
* inferred data
* generated suggestions

Never fabricate successful actions.

Do not display:

```text
Email sent
```

unless the backend confirmed that the email was actually sent.

Do not display:

```text
Saved
```

before persistence has actually succeeded unless the interface explicitly uses a safe optimistic strategy.

---

# 47. Optimistic UI

Use optimistic updates only when failure is:

* unlikely
* reversible
* clearly recoverable

For sensitive operations, wait for server confirmation.

Never optimistically claim:

```text
Payment completed
Deployment successful
Account deleted
```

before confirmation.

---

# 48. Data Freshness

When data may be stale, do not present it as guaranteed current.

Where relevant expose:

* last updated time
* refresh state
* synchronization state

Do not overwrite newer server state with stale client state without conflict handling.

---

# 49. Loading States

Loading UI should preserve layout stability.

Prefer:

* stable dimensions
* subtle skeletons
* inline progress
* local loading states

Do not use full-page loading states when only one component is updating.

Do not display an empty state before loading has completed.

Wrong:

```text
No projects found
```

appearing briefly before projects load.

Correct:

```text
Loading
→ Projects
```

Skeletons should roughly represent the final structure.

Do not add excessive skeleton animation.

---

# 50. Empty States

Empty states should help users understand what happened and what they can do next.

Do not automatically generate:

* giant illustrations
* decorative graphics
* motivational filler
* excessive copy

A useful empty state usually needs:

* concise explanation
* relevant next action

Example:

```text
No API keys yet.

Create an API key to authenticate requests.

[Create API key]
```

---

# 51. Toasts

Do not use temporary toast notifications as the only location for critical information.

Critical errors, security warnings, and required actions must remain visible until resolved.

Toasts are appropriate for lightweight confirmations such as:

```text
Copied
Saved
Archived
```

---

# 52. Dialogs

Dialogs must:

* have a clear title
* explain the action
* use explicit button labels
* support keyboard navigation
* correctly manage focus
* restore focus after closing

Avoid:

```text
Yes
No
```

Prefer:

```text
Delete project
Cancel
```

Do not use a modal when inline interaction would be simpler and equally safe.

---

# 53. Navigation

Users should understand where they are.

Avoid surprising navigation.

Do not:

* unexpectedly open new tabs
* redirect after unrelated actions
* destroy unsaved work during navigation
* unexpectedly change routes after background operations

Browser back navigation should behave predictably.

---

# 54. Accessibility

Interactive UI must work with:

* mouse
* keyboard
* touch
* screen readers

Maintain:

* semantic HTML
* visible focus states
* accessible labels
* sufficient contrast
* logical tab order
* usable touch targets

Critical information must not depend only on:

* color
* hover
* animation
* visual positioning

Prefer native semantic elements before recreating behavior with generic containers.

A button should usually be a `<button>`.

A link should usually be an `<a>`.

---

# 55. Motion

Animation must communicate something useful:

* state change
* hierarchy
* spatial relationship
* feedback

Do not animate merely because animation is available.

Avoid:

* excessive spring animations
* bouncing controls
* unnecessary entrance animations
* large movements
* slow transitions

Interactions should feel immediate.

Respect:

```css
prefers-reduced-motion
```

---

# 56. Dependency Discipline

Do not install packages for functionality that can reasonably be implemented using:

* existing dependencies
* browser APIs
* small local utilities

Before adding a dependency:

1. Search the repository for an existing solution.
2. Determine whether the dependency is actually necessary.
3. Consider bundle impact.
4. Consider maintenance cost.
5. Follow existing project conventions.

Do not install an entire UI library to implement one component.

---

# 57. Performance

Do not prematurely optimize, but avoid obvious performance mistakes.

Consider:

* unnecessary rerenders
* duplicated requests
* huge client bundles
* expensive work during render
* rendering thousands of DOM nodes
* unnecessary client-side JavaScript
* large unoptimized assets

Do not add memoization everywhere without evidence.

Do not sacrifice readability for speculative micro-optimizations.

Use the framework's established server/client boundaries correctly.

---

# 58. State Management

Keep state as local as reasonably possible.

Do not place local component state into global state without reason.

Do not duplicate derived state.

Prefer deriving values from existing state when practical.

Avoid synchronization effects whose only purpose is keeping two representations of the same state aligned.

State ownership should be obvious.

---

# 59. Effects and Side Effects

Use effects intentionally.

Do not use effects as a default mechanism for deriving state.

Avoid chains of effects that trigger one another.

Side effects should have clear ownership and cleanup.

Network mutations should happen because of explicit application events, not accidental render behavior.

---

# 60. Type Safety

Do not bypass the type system merely to make code compile.

Avoid unnecessary:

```ts
any
```

unsafe casts and ignored type errors.

Prefer accurate domain types.

Handle nullable and optional data intentionally.

Do not assume API data is valid merely because TypeScript says it is.

Validate untrusted boundaries where appropriate.

---

# 61. Naming

Names should explain intent.

Avoid:

```text
data
item
thing
temp
x
handleThing
doStuff
```

when a precise name exists.

Prefer:

```text
selectedProjectIds
isDeleteDialogOpen
handleProjectDeletion
pendingInvitation
```

Good naming reduces the need for comments.

---

# 62. Comments

Comments should explain:

WHY

not merely:

WHAT

Avoid generated-looking comments such as:

```ts
// Set loading to true
setLoading(true);
```

Use comments for:

* architectural reasoning
* non-obvious constraints
* unusual browser behavior
* important tradeoffs
* security considerations

Do not narrate obvious code.

---

# 63. No Fake Completeness

Never fake functionality to make the UI appear finished.

Do not:

* create buttons that do nothing
* fake backend success
* fake loading behavior
* hardcode successful states
* silently use mock data as real data
* create placeholder interactions without making their status clear

If backend functionality is unavailable, keep the limitation explicit during development.

A polished fake implementation is worse than an honest incomplete one.

---

# 64. Do Not Over-Engineer

Senior engineering does not mean maximum abstraction.

Do not introduce:

* factories
* registries
* providers
* reducers
* event buses
* complex generic systems

for simple local problems.

Use the least complex architecture that cleanly solves the actual requirement.

Complexity must earn its existence.

---

# 65. Do Not Under-Engineer

Do not take shortcuts that create obvious future problems.

Avoid:

* duplicated business logic
* giant components
* hardcoded domain values
* missing error handling
* ignored accessibility
* magic constants
* unsafe mutations
* fragile CSS hacks

Simple does not mean careless.

---

# 66. Preserve Scope

When implementing a feature:

Do not casually change:

* global typography
* global spacing
* navigation
* unrelated components
* component APIs
* application architecture
* dependency versions

unless required.

Senior engineering minimizes unnecessary change surface.

A focused diff is usually preferable to a broad rewrite.

---

# 67. Existing Behavior

Before replacing existing behavior, understand why it exists.

Do not remove:

* validation
* authorization checks
* confirmations
* accessibility behavior
* loading states
* error handling
* analytics hooks
* compatibility workarounds

simply because they appear unnecessary.

If uncertain about a non-obvious existing behavior, investigate before deleting it.

---

# 68. UI Safety Decision Rule

Before implementing an interaction ask:

1. Can this cause data loss?
2. Can it affect another person?
3. Can it create financial cost?
4. Can it expose private data?
5. Can it change security or permissions?
6. Can it affect production systems?
7. Is it difficult to undo?

If every answer is NO:

implement normally.

If any answer is YES:

apply proportional safeguards.

---

# 69. Stop and Ask When Necessary

Do not invent security-critical behavior.

Ask for clarification when requirements are genuinely ambiguous regarding:

* authentication
* authorization
* billing
* deletion
* privacy
* permissions
* data retention
* production deployments
* external communication
* irreversible actions

Do not ask unnecessary questions when the safe intended behavior is already clear.

Ask precise questions, not broad ones.

---

# 70. Definition of Done

A UI task is not complete until relevant states have been considered.

### Interaction states

* default
* hover
* focus
* active
* disabled

### Data states

* loading
* empty
* success
* error

### Form states

* untouched
* partial
* invalid
* submitting
* submission failure
* submission success

### Destructive action states

* confirmation
* cancellation
* processing
* failure
* success
* recovery or undo

Do not implement only the happy path.

---

# 71. Senior Developer Self-Review

Before finishing, review the implementation as if reviewing another senior engineer's pull request.

## Architecture

Ask:

* Does this follow existing project patterns?
* Did I introduce unnecessary architecture?
* Did I duplicate existing functionality?
* Are responsibilities clear?
* Is state owned by the correct component/system?

## Code Quality

Ask:

* Could another developer understand this quickly?
* Are names precise?
* Are components appropriately scoped?
* Are there magic values?
* Are there unnecessary effects?
* Are there hacks that should be replaced by normal layout or architecture?

## Visual Quality

Ask:

* Is the hierarchy obvious?
* Is anything unnecessarily decorative?
* Is spacing consistent?
* Is alignment precise?
* Did I overuse cards?
* Did I overuse borders?
* Did I overuse shadows?
* Did I overuse pills?
* Did I overuse accent colors?

## Interaction

Ask:

* Are actions predictable?
* Are loading states handled?
* Are errors handled?
* Are destructive operations protected?
* Can actions accidentally execute twice?
* Is user input preserved?

## Responsive

Ask:

* Does this work at narrow widths?
* Does content overflow?
* Are important actions still discoverable?
* Are touch targets usable?
* Does the mobile layout preserve information priority?

## Accessibility

Ask:

* Can this be operated with a keyboard?
* Is focus visible?
* Are controls semantically correct?
* Do icon-only controls have accessible names?
* Is critical state communicated without relying only on color?

## Security

Ask:

* Is authorization enforced server-side?
* Could sensitive information leak?
* Are dangerous defaults avoided?
* Does the UI claim success only after real success?

## Product Quality

Ask:

* Does every visible element have a purpose?
* Does this feel like production software?
* Does this match the rest of the product?
* Does it look intentionally designed rather than generated?
* Would I approve this implementation in a senior-level code review?

If an important answer is NO:

fix it before considering the task complete.

---

# 72. Final Quality Gate

Before declaring a UI task complete, verify:

* Existing project patterns were inspected first.
* Existing components were reused where appropriate.
* No unnecessary dependencies were introduced.
* No unnecessary abstractions were introduced.
* No unrelated redesign was performed.
* No arbitrary styling was introduced without reason.
* Visual hierarchy is clear.
* Spacing and alignment are consistent.
* Responsive behavior works.
* Realistic data is handled.
* Empty states are handled.
* Loading states are handled.
* Error states are handled.
* User input survives recoverable failures.
* Destructive actions cannot happen accidentally.
* Duplicate mutations are prevented.
* Sensitive information is protected.
* Authorization is not delegated to the frontend.
* Keyboard navigation works.
* Focus states are visible.
* Semantic HTML is used.
* Critical information is not color-only.
* AI suggestions are not silently executed.
* The UI never claims success without actual confirmation.
* No fake functionality was added.
* The resulting code is understandable and maintainable.

If any important item fails, fix it before finishing.

---

# 73. Default UI Philosophy

When explicit design direction does not exist, default to:

* restrained rather than flashy
* simple rather than clever
* structured rather than decorative
* consistent rather than novel
* clear rather than impressive
* functional rather than ornamental
* dense enough to be useful
* whitespace with purpose
* semantic HTML
* existing components
* existing design tokens
* clear visual hierarchy
* subtle interaction feedback

Do not attempt to make every screen visually impressive.

Good product UI often disappears behind the task the user is trying to accomplish.

The interface should feel like one coherent product designed by one experienced team.

It should never look like an AI generated a collection of fashionable components and connected them until the page looked finished.
